focus id

prism @(id) {
  focus prism
  project prism
  split prism
  shift prism
  settle prism
}

prism @prism {
  focus focus
  project project
  split split
  shift shift
  settle settle
}

abstract io tick(type) -> tock(type) { \ }

project in(prism)
project out(prism)

# Parametric type forms — the five operations applied at the type layer.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-parametric-types-and-fp-heritage.md:
# the same five verbs that act on values also act on types, with the
# same names one universe up. Two are declared here as first-class
# parametric forms; the other three (focus(T), project(T), split(T))
# fall out of the existing kernel forms (focus is type(id); project is
# field selection; split is the | disjunction in `type x = a | b`).
#
# shift(T): annotation-only parametric. Same bytes, different declared
# shape. Functor-shaped (basis transformation on the Hilbert space H):
# shift satisfies identity (shift id = id) and composition
# (shift (f . g) = shift f . shift g). The runtime cost is zero by
# construction — the type checker distinguishes T(U1) from T(U2) via
# content address; the bytes don't change. Per the Connes spectral
# triple's algebra A, shift is the change-of-coordinates operation.
#
# settle(T): verified-construction parametric. Building a settle(T)
# value IS the proof that the construction settled (monad-close /
# measurement collapse). Monad-shaped: settle satisfies left identity,
# right identity, and associativity. Content-addressing gives
# associativity for free at composition time (oid concatenation is
# associative; the substrate enforces it).
#
# Both are type-layer applications of the existing five Prism
# operations. No new primitives — the algebra is closed at five.
# The @epistemologic/property/laws/{functor,monad} grammars
# verify the laws structurally.
shift shift(type) -> type { \ }
settle settle(type) -> type { \ }

out in
out id
out @
out @prism
out shift
out settle
